{"candidates":[{"claim":"A panel of trained non-experts using a checklist will be more reliable on average than a single expert using intuition because aggregated independent judgment cancels individual noise, but the single-evaluation signature distinguishing genuine reliability from mere compliance is error decorrelation: genuine reasoning yields errors uncorrelated with the checklist's blind spots, while mere compliance yields errors clustered exactly where the checklist is silent or wrong.","mechanism":"Checklist-guided non-experts decompose a judgment into independently checkable sub-questions. When each member applies the checklist independently, their residual errors are largely idiosyncratic and uncorrelated; aggregation cancels this noise (diversity prediction theorem). A single expert's intuition, by contrast, is subject to systematic biases (anchoring, overconfidence) that aggregation cannot remove. Genuine reliability gain is signed by errors that are randomly distributed across cases and unrelated to checklist structure. Mere compliance is signed by errors that cluster at checklist boundaries—cases the checklist did not cover or rules that misfired—producing structured, correlated error patterns.","counterconditions":["If panel members' errors are correlated (e.g., they share the same checklist blind spot), aggregation fails to cancel noise and the panel offers no reliability advantage over the expert.","If the task domain is so holistic that checklist decomposition is infeasible, non-experts cannot apply the checklist meaningfully and the expert's intuition dominates.","If the single expert's intuition already internalizes the checklist's verifiable steps, the comparison collapses and the panel adds no marginal reliability.","If the evaluation only measures final accuracy and not the structure of errors, compliance and genuine reliability are empirically indistinguishable in a single evaluation."],"evidence_refs":[],"optional_refs":["SRC_006","SRC_005"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.9},{"claim":"The panel-versus-expert reliability comparison is empirically undecidable in a single evaluation because any observed superior accuracy of the panel can be attributed to either genuine reasoning or mere compliance, and these hypotheses make identical point predictions for aggregate accuracy; only the structure of errors can partially separate them, but even error structure is confounded by checklist quality.","mechanism":"In a single evaluation, both 'genuine reliability gain' and 'mere compliance that tracks the right answer' predict that the panel will match or beat the expert on verifiable outcomes. The distinction only becomes empirically tractable if we observe error patterns: compliance predicts structured errors aligned with checklist blind spots, genuine reasoning predicts dispersed errors. But this separation itself depends on the checklist having identifiable, observable blind spots. If the checklist's blind spots are unknown or if it happens to cover the test distribution perfectly, the two hypotheses are observationally equivalent within that evaluation.","counterconditions":["If the evaluation includes held-out cases deliberately constructed to probe known checklist blind spots, error structure becomes diagnostic and the two hypotheses can be separated.","If the panel's error pattern is random even on cases outside the checklist's coverage, this is evidence of genuine reasoning beyond mere compliance and the undecidability claim fails.","If the expert's errors are also structured (by systematic bias), then comparing error structures between panel and expert can distinguish genuine reliability from both compliance and expert bias.","If a within-evaluation proxy for reasoning—such as member-level justification transcripts coded for rule-recitation versus independent reasoning—correlates with decorrelated error patterns, the single evaluation gains discriminative power."],"evidence_refs":[],"optional_refs":["SRC_005","SRC_007","SRC_008"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75},{"claim":"The single-evaluation signature that distinguishes genuine reliability from mere compliance is inter-member error decorrelation on cases the checklist does not cover: if panel members disagree with each other and produce dispersed errors on uncovered cases, they are reasoning independently; if they agree with each other and fail identically on uncovered cases, they are merely complying with a shared rule and the reliability gain is compliance-tracking, not genuine.","mechanism":"Decompose the test set into cases the checklist explicitly addresses and cases it does not. On covered cases, both genuine reasoning and mere compliance produce high accuracy. On uncovered cases, genuine reasoning manifests as independent judgment: members disagree, errors are dispersed, and aggregation may still recover the right answer through diversity. Mere compliance manifests as correlated failure: members apply the nearest checklist rule, make the same error, and aggregation cannot help because the errors are perfectly correlated. The diagnostic is the intra-panel error correlation coefficient on uncovered cases, not overall accuracy.","counterconditions":["If the checklist covers the entire test distribution, there are no 'uncovered cases' and the diagnostic cannot be computed within the evaluation.","If panel members reason independently but share the same background misconception, they will produce correlated errors on uncovered cases without it being mere compliance, confounding the diagnostic.","If the single expert also fails identically on uncovered cases, the panel's correlated failure is indistinguishable from expert failure and the comparison provides no relative discrimination.","If the definition of 'covered' versus 'uncovered' is itself ambiguous or post hoc, the classification can be gamed to manufacture the appearance of decorrelation."],"evidence_refs":[],"optional_refs":["SRC_006","SRC_007"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7},{"claim":"The claim that non-expert panels are more reliable than single experts is false for tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes, because objectivity means the ground truth is checkable; in such domains, the checklist is a lossy compression of the verification procedure itself, and a single expert who has internalized the verification logic will outperform non-experts who follow the compressed version.","mechanism":"For objectively verifiable outcomes, the ideal process is not intuition but explicit verification: checking the answer against the ground truth. A checklist encodes this verification imperfectly. An expert who has internalized the verification logic applies it with higher fidelity than non-experts applying a lossy summary. Aggregation of multiple lossy applications does not recover the information lost in the compression. Therefore the expert's single high-fidelity judgment beats the panel's aggregated low-fidelity judgments, especially on edge cases where the checklist's compression drops relevant detail.","counterconditions":["If the expert's internalized verification is subject to systematic biases that the checklist's explicit steps prevent, the panel can outperform despite the lossy compression.","If the checklist is not a lossy compression but a complete decision procedure that non-experts can apply perfectly, then the panel matches or beats the expert by eliminating individual noise through aggregation.","If the task's verification is computationally intractable to internalize (e.g., multi-factor probabilistic reasoning), the expert's intuition is no higher-fidelity than the checklist and the panel's aggregation advantage dominates.","If the evaluation measures calibration rather than accuracy, the expert's overconfidence may make the panel more reliable even if the expert's point estimates are better."],"evidence_refs":[],"optional_refs":["SRC_001","SRC_004"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.45},{"claim":"The distinguishing signature is not in the panel's errors at all but in the expert's errors: if the expert's intuition produces errors that are systematically biased in a consistent direction (e.g., overconfidence, anchoring) while the panel's errors are not, the panel's reliability gain is genuine relative to the expert even if it is partly compliance-driven; the relevant comparison is not panel-compliance vs panel-reasoning but panel vs expert, and the expert's error structure is the discriminator.","mechanism":"The question asks whether the panel is more reliable than the expert, not whether the panel reasons genuinely in an absolute sense. If the expert's errors are systematically biased and the panel's errors are not—even if the panel's error avoidance is partly due to checklist compliance—the panel is genuinely more reliable relative to the available alternative. The single-evaluation signature is therefore the expert's error bias vector: if it is non-zero and the panel's is near-zero, the panel's gain is genuine in the only comparison that matters.","counterconditions":["If the expert's errors are randomly distributed (no systematic bias), the panel's compliance-based gain is merely tracking the checklist and offers no genuine reliability improvement over an unbiased expert.","If the panel's errors are also systematically biased but in a different direction, the comparison requires weighing which bias is worse, and no clean 'genuine gain' signature exists.","If the evaluation has too few cases to estimate the expert's error bias vector reliably, the signature is not detectable in a single evaluation.","If the expert's systematic bias is induced by the task framing rather than intrinsic to intuition, the comparison is confounded: the same framing might bias the panel too if they did not use a checklist."],"evidence_refs":[],"optional_refs":["SRC_006","SRC_008"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.4},{"claim":"Genuine reliability gain and mere checklist compliance are not separable within a single evaluation because they are the same phenomenon viewed from different temporal vantage points: compliance that tracks the right answer within the evaluation is genuine reliability within that evaluation, and the distinction only becomes meaningful prospectively when the evaluation distribution shifts; consequently, the search for a within-evaluation signature is a category error.","mechanism":"Reliability is always defined relative to a distribution of cases. Within a single evaluation, the distribution is fixed. If the panel's checklist-compliant process produces the right answers on this distribution, it is reliable on this distribution by definition. The question 'is this genuine reliability or mere compliance?' presupposes a future distribution on which the two might diverge—but that future distribution is absent from the single evaluation. Therefore no within-evaluation evidence can distinguish them, because the distinction is not a property of the single evaluation at all.","counterconditions":["If one can construct within the evaluation a controlled distribution shift (e.g., held-out cases from a different sub-domain), the prospective question becomes partially testable within the evaluation and the category-error claim fails.","If error structure within the evaluation contains signatures of over-fitting to the checklist (e.g., errors exactly at rule boundaries), this is evidence of compliance rather than generalizable reasoning, and the distinction is partially detectable.","If the evaluation includes process traces (justifications, reasoning protocols) that show whether participants recite rules versus reason independently, the distinction is detectable from process data even within a single evaluation.","If the evaluation is large and diverse enough to serve as a proxy for the 'future distribution,' the within-evaluation performance already tests generalization and the category-error argument collapses."],"evidence_refs":[],"optional_refs":["SRC_005","SRC_003"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.35}],"scratch_proposal":{"new_blocks":[{"body":{"bears_on_refs":["SRC_006","SRC_007","SRC_008"],"content":"Counterfactual probe: construct pairs of evaluation cases that are identical on all checklist-relevant features but differ on a feature the checklist does not encode. If panel accuracy differs between the paired cases, members are using information beyond the checklist (genuine reasoning). If accuracy is identical, they are pure compliance. This creates a within-evaluation experimental design that directly tests the compliance-vs-reasoning distinction.","possible_next_move":"Design a simulation that generates paired cases with checklist-relevant features held constant and a checklist-irrelevant feature varied, then compare panel accuracy on the paired cases.","why_keep_this":"This is a concrete experimental design that could resolve the debate about whether a single evaluation can distinguish genuine reliability from mere compliance."},"local_key":"NEW_001"}],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"If the paired-case design reveals that panel members use information beyond the checklist, does that constitute 'genuine reasoning' or could it still be a form of learned pattern-matching that is not generalizable?","related_refs":["NEW_001","SRC_006"]},{"question":"Can the paired-case counterfactual probe be implemented in real evaluation settings without the checklist authors being able to anticipate and encode the 'irrelevant' features?","related_refs":["NEW_001"]}]}}